{"kind":"request","text":"Add local-first performance instrumentation that can later serve a containerized Gitseq server, governed by the performance evidence contract in the parent request. OpenTelemetry is the transport and shared vocabulary, not the measurement discipline itself. Keep telemetry disabled or no-op by default and wire SDK configuration and export at command and service composition boundaries so the semantic-free kernel does not acquire Workroom or deployment meaning. Add standard low-cardinality HTTP request traces and metrics, Go runtime metrics, and bounded custom observations for workspace snapshot source and duration, cache or shared-flight behavior, checkpoint load and fallback, kernel verification phases, verified and tail event counts, fold and status-view construction, JSON encoding, submission phases and retries, long-poll wake behavior, and Git subprocess count, duration, and outcome by a finite operation family. Aggregate large scans rather than emitting one span per event. Support explicit local OTLP export to a Collector or test exporter and leave a documented OTLP path for a sidecar or adjacent Collector in a container deployment. Keep pprof and Go execution, block, and mutex profiling as separate controlled diagnostics. Do not put event IDs, heads, genesis IDs, actors, repository paths, branches, conversations, payload text, or idempotency keys in metric labels; raw URL paths are not route labels. Measure disabled and enabled overhead with the performance lane before claiming the instrumentation is cheap.","body":{"branch":"request/performance-instrumentation","conditions":"Telemetry is disabled or no-op by default and does not change command, HTTP, audit, submission, or fallback results; SDK and exporter ownership stays at command or service composition layers while kernel, app, Git storage, fold, and status view expose only neutral observation seams; HTTP telemetry uses route templates and finite method, status, and error attributes; custom spans and metrics distinguish exact cache, incremental tail, checkpoint tail, and cold audit and expose bounded verification, checkpoint, fold, encoding, submit, wait, and Git-operation costs without per-event span growth; runtime heap, allocation, garbage-collection, goroutine, and scheduler evidence is available; local OTLP or in-memory export is documented and tested and the container contract names an OTLP Collector boundary without requiring a production backend; high-cardinality identifiers and payload content are absent from metric dimensions and are sampled or omitted from traces by an explicit privacy policy; profiler endpoints are absent by default and restricted when enabled; the parent evidence lane measures disabled and enabled overhead and records any material regression; architecture review names the affected layers and updates the canonical architecture page in the same head if its contract changes; implementation is on the named request branch and receives independent exact-head architecture, security, and simplification review before merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}